{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Phase 1 RCA — confirmed root cause, and a correction to the reported blast radius\n\n### Proven mechanism\nscripts/gen_env_registry.py::scan recognises exactly three direct stdlib shapes -- the\nos.environ[...] subscript, os.environ.get(...) (the func.attr == \"get\" test, now at :98\nafter the b00f edit shifted it), and os.getenv(...) -- plus the KNOWN_ENV_HELPERS table.\nEvery other expression falls through the `for node in ast.walk(tree)` loop with no branch\nand no diagnostic. The fallthrough is the defect: an unrecognised os.environ access is a\nsilent skip, so the key it reads is simply absent from the generated registry and the\ndrift gate stays green because the doc still agrees with the (blind) generator.\n\n### Discriminating experiment (dynamic -- run, not read)\nSwapped opcert_service/config.py:102 from os.environ.get(\"REBAR_OPCERT_PRIVATE_KEY\") to\nos.environ.pop(\"REBAR_OPCERT_PRIVATE_KEY\", None) -- the one-token edit the ticket\ndescribes -- then ran the generator:\n\n  before: _162 variables._   REBAR_OPCERT_PRIVATE_KEY present\n  after:  _161 variables._   REBAR_OPCERT_PRIVATE_KEY absent (grep count 0)\n  generator exit code: 0 in both runs\n\nThat reproduces the reported 1-key drop exactly, and confirms the silence: nothing on\nstdout, stderr, or the exit code distinguishes the two runs. Source restored; git diff\nclean. This rules out the alternative that the key survives via another read site.\n\n### Correction: ZERO keys are being dropped today, not one\nThe ticket's headline instance is already mitigated, and the audit found no second one.\nA full AST census of every os.environ attribute access under src/rebar returns exactly\nthree attributes in use:\n\n  os.environ.get      115 sites  (recognised)\n  os.environ.pop        2 sites  (NOT recognised)\n  os.environ.copy       1 site   (bulk, no literal key -- nothing to register)\n\nBoth pop sites read a key that is ALSO reached by a recognised shape in the same module,\nso neither is currently lost:\n\n  - opcert_service/config.py:106 pops REBAR_OPCERT_PRIVATE_KEY, but :102 still reads it\n    with os.environ.get -- the deliberate workaround illusory-patronal-giraffe left in\n    place, with a comment saying so.\n  - _io/import_ndjson.py:393 pops REBAR_SYNC_PUSH, but :216 reads it with\n    os.environ.get and :217/:395 assign it by subscript.\n\nAlso checked and found absent: any `x = os.environ` alias binding (none), and any\nsetdefault/getitem-with-default form (none). The five os.environ pass-by-reference sites\n(dict(os.environ), project_child_env(os.environ, ...), raise_or_warn_env(os.environ)) are\nbulk handoffs with no literal key, so there is nothing for the registry to record.\n\nSo AC4 is already satisfied and the count has since GROWN to 162, not 161. The defect is\nLATENT, not active: the blind spot is real and reproducible on demand, but it is currently\ncosting zero documented keys. Fixing it is prophylactic -- which is the right call, since\nthe next pop-based read hits it without the giraffe ticket's luck.\n\n### Why this is a defect and not the documented scope limit\nThe generator's docstring does disclaim exhaustive indirection coverage. Two authorities\noverride that for this case:\n1. Its own stated invariant, same docstring: \"a CI drift gate regenerates it and fails the\n   build on any diff, so a new env-var read cannot ship undocumented.\" A pop-based read\n   ships undocumented AND green. The invariant is violated, not merely unfulfilled.\n2. Prior closed Fixed: bug b00f-c62e-d3b9-4e63 (scared-restored-swallow, closed\n   2026-08-01) is the same class -- a read shape invisible to the scanner producing\n   undocumented vars -- and was accepted as a defect then. Its own commit message records\n   the class as still open: \"A missing row is not an error, it is invisible ... a clean\n   --check proves the doc AGREES with the generator, never that it is COMPLETE.\"\n\nClassification: defect (specifically an omission -- a guard that was never written).\n\n### Defect origin -- caused_by 0f21-ca03-f063-4562\ngit blame -w -C -M -L 95,102 puts the recogniser on c1f17861ec2d9ea2b55b82990dba22cda446d74b\n(2026-07-08, \"feat(maint): generated REBAR_* env-var registry with a CI drift gate\",\nrebar-ticket: 0f21-ca03-f063-4562). Confirmed against the commit's own blob: the narrow\nrecogniser and the silent fallthrough are present in the file's FIRST version, so there is\nno carrier commit to walk through and no good->bad transition to bisect.\n\nDate test applied: the implicated lines do not predate a transition, because there was no\ntransition -- the behaviour was never right. This is the omission case, so the attribution\nis by introducing commit AND by scope of work: 0f21's plan enumerated the read-pattern set\nand chose a silent skip for everything else, and 0f21's own acceptance language (\"a new\nenv-var read cannot ship undocumented\") is the invariant this gap breaks. A reviewer on\n0f21 could plausibly have caught it.\n\nConsidered and rejected as the origin: b00f-c62e-d3b9-4e63. It touched the same file most\nrecently (ecb9149412, 2026-08-01) and a naive whole-file blame majority leans that way,\nbut it only ADDED a KNOWN_ENV_HELPERS row -- it did not create the fallthrough and did not\nmake any previously-correct behaviour wrong. Recording the divergence from the blame\ndefault per protocol. Close class will be plan_defect, not regression.\n\n### Caller-dependency sweep\nscripts/check_config_ownership.py:164 does `reads, _dynamic = scan(...)` -- the only\nnon-test consumer of scan(). The fix must preserve the 2-tuple arity (it will; the\nfail-closed signal is an exception, not a third element). Post-fix scan() output over the\nCURRENT tree is byte-identical, because both pop keys are already registered via their\nsibling .get reads -- so the config-ownership gate sees no change today. Incidental\ndependency only; no behavioural dependency on the current narrowness. Gate cleared."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787293159311218001,"uuid":"2dda69bf-296e-44cc-b865-093a271033c5"}